[{"id":91088,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91088\">#1</a> At <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computational%20trilogy\">computational trilogy</a></em> I made explicit the quantum version – <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computational+trilogy#QuantumParametrized\">here</a>. </p>\n<p>As with the classical trilogy, it’s evident once you think about it.</p>","timestamp":1620927803,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":91091,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91091\">#2</a> Can we think of anything as neat as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>A <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/proof\">proof</a> of a <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/proposition\">proposition</a>. (In <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/logic\">logic</a>.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/program\">program</a> with output some <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/type\">type</a>. (In <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/type%20theory\">type theory</a> and <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computer%20science\">computer science</a>.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/generalized%20element\">generalized element</a> of an <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/object\">object</a>. (In <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/category%20theory\">category theory</a>.)</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Something to do with your <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/propositions%20as%20projections\">propositions as projections</a> idea?</p>","timestamp":1620934097,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":91092,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91092\">#3</a> Yes, it’s exactly the same dictionary. Just that the types/objects/spaces in the fibers are now linear.</p>\n<p>(And we notice that for “generalized element” we may equivalently say “cocycle”  in all cases: classical, parameterized, quantum.)</p>","timestamp":1620935349,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":91096,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91096\">#4</a> In fact, without the parametrization/control-aspect, the quantum trilogy is already  Melliès’s main point, on his <a href=\"https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00154243/document#page=5\">p. 4</a> – I have tried to bring that out more clearly in the entry now, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computational+trilogy#QuantumPlain\">here</a></p>","timestamp":1620976977,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":91097,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91097\">#5</a> Perhaps a little speculative for this time in the morning, but if we follow Susskind in </p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Copenhagen vs Everett, Teleportation, and ER=EPR\nLeonard Susskind</em>, (<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.02589\">arXiv:1604.02589</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The universe\nis filled with subsystems, any one of which can play the role of observer. There is no place\nin the laws of quantum mechanics for wave function collapse; the only thing that happens\nis that the overall wave function evolves unitarily and becomes more and more entangled.\nThe universe is an immensely complicated network of entangled subsystems, and only in\nsome approximation can we single out a particular subsystem as THE OBSERVER.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>is there are quantum trilogy which is not classically controlled? Or is it that just ‘quantum plain’?</p>","timestamp":1620979354,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":91098,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91098\">#6</a> Let me lay out the picture that I have at the moment, also so that you see where <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Fundamental%20weight%20systems%20are%20quantum%20states\">you fit in</a>:</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Hypothesis H, full quantum version</strong>:</p>\n<p>For </p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>-orbi <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">32</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^{10,1\\vert \\mathbf{32}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.888em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.888em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">10</span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1∣</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathbf mtight\">32</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>-fold <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1464em;\">X</span></span></span></span> (spacetime)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>with geometric torsion-free <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Spin}(10,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Spin</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>-structure (<a href=\"https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/11d-gravity-just-torsion-constraint/\">i.e.</a> field of super-gravity) </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>further lifted to tangential <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sp}(2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Sp</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>-structure <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span></span></span></span> (anomaly cancellation), </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>consider the <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cocycle%20space\">cocycle space</a> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">π</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{\\pi}_G^\\tau(\\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0253em;vertical-align:-0.2753em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4247em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">G</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2753em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1464em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span></span></span></span>-twisted equivariant differential 4-Cohomotopy (<a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Hypothesis%20H\">i.e.</a> the C-field configuration <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/G-space\">G-space</a>) </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>and then the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>-equivariant <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span>-cohomology cocycle space of its loop space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">E</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">π</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{E}^\\bullet_G\\big( \\Omega \\mathbf{\\pi}_G^\\tau(\\mathcal{X})  \\big)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2em;vertical-align:-0.35em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">E</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6741em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4247em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">G</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∙</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2753em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"delimsizing size1\">(</span></span><span class=\"mord\">Ω</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4247em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">G</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2753em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1464em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"delimsizing size1\">)</span></span></span></span></span> (quantum states)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>regarded as a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>-equivariant spectrum (e.g. with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E = H\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1464em;\">X</span></span></span></span> a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>⊥</mo><mi>D</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D6 \\perp D8</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span><span class=\"mord\">8</span></span></span></span> spacetime as in <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Differential%20Cohomotopy%20implies%20intersecting%20brane%20observables\">SS19</a> this has homotopy groups the <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Fundamental%20weight%20systems%20are%20quantum%20states\">weight systems on horizontal chord diagrams</a>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>hence as a <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/spectral%20Mackey%20functor\">spectral Mackey functor</a> </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>hence as a parameterized spectrum (over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>-sets) equipped with consistent orientation for pull/push transformations</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>hence as a cohomological QFT-via-secondary-integral-transforms in the sense of Sec. 7 of <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Quantization%20via%20Linear%20homotopy%20types\">Quantization via Linear homotopy types</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unwinding the definitions, this QFT encodes </p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>quantum operations between quantum states of C-field configurations </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>associated with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span></span></span></span>-fixed loci of the original spacetime orbifold <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1464em;\">X</span></span></span></span>. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>But since these loci are conical black brane loci (as in <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Equivariant%20homotopy%20and%20super%20M-branes\">HSS 18</a>) in the spacetime, </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>we see that we have a “classically controlled quantum system” where the classical control parameter says where in a holographic system we are: </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>in the bulk (<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>-fixed locus) </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>or on a brane (<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span></span></span></span>-fixed locus) </p>\n<ul>\n<li>and if so on which of possibly several intersecting ones (choice of subgroup <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H \\subset G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7224em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊂</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p>Now take this mathematical picture and compare to the current folklore in string theory. Within the large hand-waving error bars of the latter, this could match well, I think.</p>","timestamp":1620982744,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":91123,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91123\">#7</a> Quite a vision! </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>we see that we have a “classically controlled quantum system” where the classical control parameter says where in a holographic system we are</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think this is the part I’ve heard least about before. </p>\n<p>I remember you giving an abstract general nPOV on holography, way back. Yes, here at <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/holographic%20principle%20of%20higher%20category%20theory\">holographic principle of higher category theory</a>. Untouched for nearly a decade.</p>","timestamp":1621159089,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":102429,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/102429\">#8</a> Could one say then that <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quantum%20computation\">quantum computation</a> as well as any experimental observation in quantum physics, such as what occurs at the LHC, is all covered by this concept of “classically controlled quantum system” as in <a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91098\">#6</a>?</p>\n<p>Perhaps this is the direction <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Physics%20of%20the%20Observer\">Physics of the Observer (schreiber)</a> would have tended in, had it been funded.</p>","timestamp":1667124162,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":102434,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/102434\">#9</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Could one say then that <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quantum%20computation\">quantum computation</a> as well as any experimental observation in quantum physics, such as what occurs at the LHC, is all covered by this concept of “classically controlled quantum system” </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Certainly quantum computation and all “quantum protocols”. I have a new page with a list of claims, here: <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/QS\">QS (schreiber)</a></em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>as in <a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91098\">#6</a>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The comment in <a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91098\">#6</a> above was about a different aspect, namely the aspect of holography. We hope to come back to this when some more practical details have been laid out.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Perhaps this is the direction <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Physics%20of%20the%20Observer\">Physics of the Observer (schreiber)</a> would have tended in, had it been funded.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I had had the feeling that we “just” need to see linear projections onto eigenstates arise as categorical semantics of an evident logical construct on linear types in order for all quantum mystery to dissolve. It was after a discussion with David J. M. and Mitchell R. over lunch some weeks back that I suddently saw (<a href=\"#narrow/channel/2/topic/necessity.20and.20possibility/near/101594\">here</a>) that the collapse projections are right there in the necessity counit on dependent linear types. In hindsight it seems so obvious now! (Yoneda-lemma style)</p>","timestamp":1667150570,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":102449,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/102449\">#10</a> This looks very interesting. Just the kind of thing Friedman would expect if quantum physics is to be properly integrated philosophically. He himself point to quantum logic as the kind of advance needed. It seems we had to wait several decades for linear HoTT.</p>\n<p>I was just thinking from <a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Quantum.20Computational.20Trilogy/near/91098\">#6</a>, where you wrote</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>we see that we have a “classically controlled quantum system” where the classical control parameter says where in a holographic system we are,</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>that when we talked once a while ago about how a theory of quantum gravity by itself has no notion of an observer, that any account of observation of the world must be framed with some kind of classical interface.</p>","timestamp":1667205316,"subject":"Quantum Computational Trilogy","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]